  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring the lottery with the *lower* probability of its maximum outcome (i.e., prefer B if 33% < 34%). The relevant information—probabilities of maximum outcomes (33% vs. 34%)—is present in the problem and is mentioned by the individual (33% vs. 34%). However, the individual does not use this information to prefer the lower probability; instead, they treat the probabilities as nearly equal and then base their decision on the monetary amount. Their justification is about wanting more money ('100 EURO mehr'), implying a focus on outcome size, not on avoiding higher probabilities of maximum outcomes. There is no indication that they prefer a lottery *because* it has a lower chance of delivering its top prize. In fact, they seem to prefer the lottery with the higher probability of the maximum outcome (34% for A) and possibly believe it offers a higher amount (which it does not). Thus, while the probabilities are acknowledged, the rule of the decision reason (prefer lower probability of max outcome) is not followed, nor is it part of the justification. The individual’s reasoning is inconsistent with the decision reason’s logic.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

